  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 334 AM EDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nRidging will continue to slide over the Great Lakes today, \neventually pushing east of the region by the back half of the \nweekend as troughing progresses overhead. The primary shortwave will \npass just to our north as it crosses Lake Superior/Ontario on Sunday \nwith a second, convectively-augmented shortwave set to track across \nthe far southern Great Lakes/Ohio Valley. Elongated low pressure \nstretching from Hudson Bay down to the mid Mississippi Valley will \nbe supported by the aforementioned features and trek eastward on \nSunday. Surface high pressure looks to build in behind this to start \nnext week. Confidence continues to increase in troughing settling \ninto the region during the middle of next week.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nThe primary forecast concern is the return of rain chances and \nperhaps a few storms this weekend. Latest observations and guidance \ncontinue to push back anticipated arrival time for said chances, as \nwell as decreased confidence in more widespread rain chances -- \nwhich has been discussed in previous forecast cycles. Aforementioned \nsplit support aloft contributes to this, and is expected to support \nbetter rain/storm chances to our north and south both today and \nSunday. While cloud cover will begin to increase across the area \ntoday, dry weather is anticipated across northern Michigan into this \nevening. Low chances exist for showers near M-55 and across the \neastern U.P. overnight, but our best rain chances will come on \nSunday. While coverage appears it will be less than once thought for \nthis weekend, scattered showers and a few storms are still expected \nlater Sunday morning into Sunday evening. Brief heavy downpours and \nlightning will be the primary concerns, and severe weather is not \nexpected.\n\nAfter rain chances come to an end Sunday night, dry weather is \nexpected to start the week as high pressure builds in. Seasonable \nhighs in the upper 70s to low 80s are also anticipated to continue \nthrough the first half of next week. Our next round of rain chances \nwill move in by the middle of next week as troughing works into the \nregion, also bringing some cooler temperatures through the end of \nthe forecast period.",
